      "tag": "main",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "h1",
          "children": [
            "Atomicity, recovery reasoning, and two-session concurrency"
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "p",
          "children": [
            "Meetings 23–25. Open two independent PostgreSQL connections A and B to the same teaching database. In each use ",
            {
              "tag": "code",
              "children": [
                "SET search_path TO course32;"
              ]
            },
            ". Finish every experiment with COMMIT or ROLLBACK before beginning the next."
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            "Meeting 23: atomic transfer and simplified WAL"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "ol",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Read the accounts: 1=100, 2=100. BEGIN; debit account 1 by 25; credit account 2 by 25; COMMIT. Verify 75,125 and conserved total 200."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Begin another transfer but attempt a negative balance. Record the error and ROLLBACK. Verify the committed balances survive and the failed transaction makes no partial change."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Reset to 100 each using a committed UPDATE when both sessions are idle."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "p",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "strong",
              "children": [
                "Paper recovery model:"
              ]
            },
            " Assume steal/no-force, undo/redo logging with before/after values, and a durable log prefix. Initial A=100, B=100. Durable log: START T1; T1 A:100→80; T1 B:100→120; COMMIT T1; START T2; T2 A:80→70; CRASH. Disk at crash has A=70,B=100. Identify winner/loser, redo committed updates and undo incomplete updates, and derive the recovered state. Explain why the log must be durable before an affected page is flushed. This is a teaching model, not a claim that PostgreSQL uses this exact undo algorithm."
          ]
        },
        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            "Meeting 24: schedules and deadlock"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "p",
          "children": [
            "For S1 = r1(A), w1(A), r2(A), w2(B), c1, c2, draw the precedence graph and discuss serializability separately from recoverability. For S2 = r1(A), r2(B), w1(B), w2(A), draw the graph and find the cycle."
          ]
        },
        "\n",
