        "\n",
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            "Meeting 25: snapshots and guarded writes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "ol",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "A: BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED; SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id=1. B: update that account by +10 and commit. A: repeat the SELECT, then ROLLBACK. Record the new value."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Repeat with A at REPEATABLE READ; establish its snapshot with the first SELECT before B’s committed update. A’s second ordinary SELECT keeps its earlier value."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Reset stock item 1 to quantity=1. A: BEGIN; UPDATE stock SET quantity=quantity-1 WHERE id=1 AND quantity>0 RETURNING quantity. B: issue the same update in its own transaction. After observing the wait, commit A. Under READ COMMITTED B returns no row. Commit B and verify quantity=0."
              ]
            },
            {
              "tag": "li",
              "children": [
                "Explain why reading quantity into the application and later writing an absolute value can lose updates. State what the guarded update guarantees and how the application should interpret zero affected rows."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
